## Why This Is Asked
This angle tests practical integration of real-time UX with common Azure PaaS: static hosting, serverless APIs, scalable data, and live updates, plus governance and disaster recovery.

## Key Concepts
- Azure Static Web Apps with SPA routing
- Azure Functions (Consumption) as API layer
- Cosmos DB SQL API as scalable data store
- Azure SignalR Service for real-time client updates
- Azure Entra ID for authentication
- Azure Front Door with TLS termination
- GitHub Actions for CI/CD
- Resource tagging and cost budgeting
- Cross-region DR for Cosmos DB

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example: Azure Function (JavaScript) emitting a SignalR update
module.exports = async function (context, req) {
  const update = { orderId: req.params.id, status: req.body.status };
  context.bindings.signalRMessages = [{
    target: 'orderUpdated',
    arguments: [ update ]
  }];
  context.res = { status: 200 };
};
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test latency of real-time updates across regions?
- What security practices would you apply for API keys and secrets in this stack?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to wire basic observability across static web apps, serverless functions, and a database, a foundational skill for troubleshooting in Azure.

## Key Concepts
- Application Insights integration on SPA and Function
- Correlation IDs across SPA -> Function -> SQL
- Log Analytics queries to join telemetry and database logs
- End-to-end request tracing and basic dashboards

## Code Example
```javascript
// SPA instrumentation and correlationId
import { ApplicationInsights } from "@microsoft/applicationinsights-web";
const appInsights = new ApplicationInsights({ config: { instrumentationKey: "<ikey>" } });
appInsights.loadAppInsights();
const correlationId = crypto.randomUUID();
fetch("/api/feedback", {
  method: "POST",
  headers: { "X-Correlation-Id": correlationId, "Content-Type": "application/json" },
  body: JSON.stringify({ text, rating })
});
```

```csharp
// Function: read header and insert into SQL with correlationId
string corr = req.Headers["X-Correlation-Id"];
using (var cmd = new SqlCommand("INSERT INTO Feedback (Text, CorrId) VALUES (@t, @c)", conn)) {
  cmd.Parameters.AddWithValue("@t", text);
  cmd.Parameters.AddWithValue("@c", corr);
  cmd.ExecuteNonQuery();
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you verify the correlation path in Log Analytics?
- How would you handle missing correlation IDs in edge cases?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical knowledge of observability in a beginner Azure stack, ensuring the candidate can wire instrumentation, telemetry exports, and basic dashboards end-to-end.

## Key Concepts
- OpenTelemetry integration in Azure Functions
- Application Insights exporter and per-endpoint metrics
- Sampling, traces, and dashboards in Azure Monitor
- GitHub Actions CI/CD for deployments
- Front Door TLS endpoint configuration
- SQL Auto Failover Group for cross-region DR

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal OpenTelemetry setup (Node.js)
const { NodeSDK } = require('@opentelemetry/sdk-node');
const { getNodeAutoInstrumentations } = require('@opentelemetry/auto-instrumentations-node');
const { AzureMonitorTraceExporter } = require('@opentelemetry/exporter-azure-monitor');

const sdk = new NodeSDK({
  traceExporter: new AzureMonitorTraceExporter({
    connectionString: process.env.APPLICATIONINSIGHTS_CONNECTION_STRING
  }),
  instrumentations: [getNodeAutoInstrumentations()],
  sampler: new AlwaysParentSampler(0.2) // 20% sampling
});

sdk.start();
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes practical multi-tenant governance, cross-subscription access, and automation in a real-world analytics scenario. It tests data isolation, cost control, and operational readiness.

## Key Concepts
- Azure Data Lake Gen2, Azure Synapse Spark
- Azure Lighthouse for cross-tenant access
- API Management as gateway
- Azure Policy, RBAC, tagging, encryption
- Terraform + GitHub Actions CI/CD, Cost Management budgets

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: create Lighthouse assignment (illustrative)
const { DefaultAzureCredential } = require('@azure/identity');
const { LighthouseClient } = require('@azure/arm-lighthouse');
async function createAssignment() {
  const cred = new DefaultAzureCredential();
  const client = new LighthouseClient(cred, '<subscription-id>');
  await client.assignments.createOrUpdate('<scope>', '<assignment-name>', {
    properties: {
      principalId: '<principal-id>',
      roleDefinitionId: '/providers/Microsoft.Authorization/roleDefinitions/...'
    }
  });
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate cross-tenant access boundaries and revoke access when needed?
- What monitoring and alerting would you implement to catch misconfigurations early?
